 common mistakes that lead to compile-time or run-time errors.\\n\\nA good way to study the if else statement is to connect the rule with a small program. Java is strict about syntax and types, so every declaration, expression and block must be written carefully. When a concept uses code, the code should be short, readable and self-contained.\\n\\n```java\\nint number = -3;\\nif (number >= 0) {\\n    System.out.println(\\\"Non-negative\\\");\\n} else {\\n    System.out.println(\\\"Negative\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIn this example, the program demonstrates the central idea of the if else statement. Notice the use of clear names, braces, semicolons and explicit types. These details matter because Java checks them before the program can run.\\n\\nA common mistake is to memorize the syntax without understanding why it is used. Instead, read the code line by line, identify the variables involved, check the condition or operation, and predict the result before running it.\\n\\nSummary: The if else statement should be learned as a practical tool. You should be able to explain the rule, recognize correct and incorrect examples, and write a small Java program that uses the concept correctly.\", \"isRead\": false, \"quotations\": [], \"images\": [], \"exercises\": [{\"type\": \"mcq\", \"difficulty\": \"medium\", \"estimatedMinutes\": 2, \"question\": \"What is the main purpose of this code related to The if else statement?\\n\\n```java\\nint number = -3;\\nif (number >= 0) {\\n    System.out.println(\\\"Non-negative\\\");\\n} else {\\n    System.out.println(\\\"Negative\\\");\\n}\\n```\", \"options\": [\"It demonstrates the use of The if else statement in Java\", \"It replaces Java syntax with pseudocode\", \"It intentionally ignores type checking\", \"It deletes all variables from memory\"], \"answerIndex\": 0, \"hint\": \"Focus on the concept being practiced and how Java checks code.\", \"explanation\": \"The unit teaches The if else statement as a specific Java concept with pr