 if else statement is understood and not only memorized.\", \"solution\": \"The if else statement is used to write clearer and more correct Java programs. A common mistake is to use the syntax mechanically without checking types, scope, braces or the expected result.\"}]}, {\"title\": \"The switch statement\", \"numbering\": 33, \"text\": \"The switch statement is an important Java topic. Select one branch among many constant cases. The goal of this unit is to understand the concept clearly, use it correctly in programs, and avoid common mistakes that lead to compile-time or run-time errors.\\n\\nA good way to study the switch statement is to connect the rule with a small program. Java is strict about syntax and types, so every declaration, expression and block must be written carefully. When a concept uses code, the code should be short, readable and self-contained.\\n\\n```java\\nint day = 2;\\nswitch (day) {\\n    case 1:\\n        System.out.println(\\\"Monday\\\");\\n        break;\\n    case 2:\\n        System.out.println(\\\"Tuesday\\\");\\n        break;\\n    default:\\n        System.out.println(\\\"Unknown\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIn this example, the program demonstrates the central idea of the switch statement. Notice the use of clear names, braces, semicolons and explicit types. These details matter because Java checks them before the program can run.\\n\\nA common mistake is to memorize the syntax without understanding why it is used. Instead, read the code line by line, identify the variables involved, check the condition or operation, and predict the result before running it.\\n\\nSummary: The switch statement should be learned as a practical tool. You should be able to explain the rule, recognize correct and incorrect examples, and write a small Java program that uses the concept correctly.\", \"isRead\": false, \"quotations\": [], \"images\": [], \"exercises\": [{\"type\": \"mcq\", \"difficulty\": \"medium\", \"estimatedMinutes\": 2, \"question\": \"